// Regression test (was CODE_REVIEW_GAPS_2026-07.md gap #2, "Generic structs
// have no valid syntax to instantiate with explicit type arguments"): a
// generic struct can now be constructed, have its fields accessed, and have
// generic methods called on it.
//
// This was fixed WITHOUT adding new Parser syntax for explicit type
// arguments on a brace literal (`Box<int> { ... }` still does not parse --
// `<` after an identifier in expression position is still just the
// less-than operator, and disambiguating it from a generic-argument list
// would need real backtracking support this hand-written recursive-descent
// parser doesn't have). Instead:
//
//   1. `Types.cpp`'s typesCompatible() now treats a bare construction's type
//      (`Box::new(...)`/`Box::default()` always produce plain
//      NamedType("Box") -- the Resolver's ConstructorCall/DefaultConstruct
//      dispatch in visitFuncCall, and TypeChecker's DefaultConstruct branch,
//      never produce a GenericType themselves; this was originally true of
//      the now-removed brace-literal form's TypeChecker::visitStructInit()
//      too, and still holds through the newer construction paths) as
//      compatible with a GenericType of the same base name (`Box<int>`) on
//      the OTHER side of an assignment/declaration. This is sound because
//      this compiler's type-erasure model gives every
//      instantiation of a generic struct the exact same LLVM layout (a
//      field typed with the struct's own type parameter is always a
//      pointer, which erases to an opaque pointer regardless of what it
//      points to) -- there is no monomorphized layout to pick between, so
//      the concrete type argument only needs to be carried by the
//      surrounding declared type, never by the literal itself.
//   2. A genuinely separate, pre-existing Parser bug was found and fixed
//      along the way: `ParseStructDecl()`'s own `GlobalSymbolTable->
//      RegisterType(Name, SType)` call used to run INSIDE the temporary
//      scope pushed for resolving the struct's generic type parameters,
//      so it was immediately un-registered the moment the scope popped at
//      the end of parsing -- meaning EVERY generic struct's own name was
//      permanently unlookupable via GlobalSymbolTable->LookupType()
//      immediately after being declared, in the SAME file, regardless of
//      how it was later referenced.
//   3. Codegen's FieldAccessExprAST (codegenLValue/codegen) and
//      TypeChecker's visitMethodCall()/visitFieldAccess() all resolved a
//      NamedType base to its real TurfStructType via GlobalSymbolTable, but
//      had no equivalent resolution for a GenericType base (e.g. a
//      `Box<int>`-typed variable) -- so field access and (much more subtly)
//      METHOD CALLS on a generic struct instance silently failed.
//      Specifically: TypeChecker::visitMethodCall()'s struct-method lookup
//      fell through to "be permissive" (Node.ResolvedCallTarget left null)
//      for any generic-struct-typed base, and Codegen's
//      MethodCallExprAST::codegen() then silently returned nullptr with NO
//      diagnostic when ResolvedCallTarget was null -- which meant a
//      variable declared from that call's result was never registered in
//      Codegen's symbol table, surfacing only much later as a totally
//      unrelated-looking "you're trying to use 'p' before telling me what
//      it is" error at the NEXT place that variable happened to be
//      referenced in a way Codegen actually reached (this specific failure
//      mode only became visible once the call was wrapped in `test::assert()`,
//      since TypeChecker's earlier, unrelated rejections in other contexts
//      had been masking it).

// (issue #238) `Box<T>.val`'s field type is `T*` -- still a raw pointer
// (isRawPointer() gates it regardless of T being an open generic param),
// so the struct decl needs `unsafe { }`. set()/get() used to spell their
// parameter/return as `T*`; a plain function may never have a raw ptr/T*
// parameter or return type, even inside `unsafe { }` (see
// checkNoRawPointersInSignature()), so they now use `&T` instead --
// reference-to-generic-param compatibility already works the identical
// way pointer-to-generic-param compatibility always has (src/Types.cpp),
// via the same inheritance that makes `&T` a `TurfPointerType` underneath.
import test;

unsafe {
    struct Box<T> {
        val: T*
    }
}

fn<T> Box.set(v: &T): void {
    self.val = v;
}

fn<T> Box.get(): &T {
    // self.val is a raw T*; converting it to the declared &T return type
    // is the raw-to-safe direction, which always needs an explicit cast
    // inside `unsafe { }` (it might have been null -- ::default() zero-
    // inits every pointer field, so in practice it's never read before
    // set() is called, but the compiler can't know that statically).
    unsafe {
        return self.val to &T;
    }
}

fn main(): int {
    // Construction + direct field access, no user-defined constructor
    // involved -- `val` is a pointer field, and ::default() already
    // zero-inits it to null, exactly the value the old brace-literal spelled
    // out explicitly.
    mut b: Box<int> = Box::default();
    test::assert(b.val == null, "");

    // Generic method call: set() takes &T (&int), get() returns &T (&int).
    mut n: int = 42;
    b.set(&n);
    mut p: &int = b.get();
    test::assert(p == &n, "");
    // No `p != null` check anymore: &T is a compiler-guaranteed non-null
    // reference, so that's now a static guarantee instead of something
    // worth asserting at runtime (and `null` isn't even assignable/
    // comparable to a `&T` -- see typesCompatible()'s Rule 1).

    // A second, independently-typed instantiation of the SAME generic
    // struct in the same file, to confirm the shared-layout model doesn't
    // cause any cross-instantiation interference.
    mut sb: Box<string> = Box::default();
    mut s: string = "hello";
    sb.set(&s);
    mut sp: &string = sb.get();
    test::assert(sp == &s, "");

    return 0;
}
